    "case_id": "578",
    "case_summary": "A 67-year-old man with a history of mood disorder, Parkinson's disease, hyperlipidemia, and mild cognitive impairment presented after an unwitnessed fall at home. Family noticed increasing weakness, fatigue, and confusion a few weeks before presentation. Patient reported decreased appetite and mild dyspnea, especially on exertion. No significant weight loss, dysphagia, or odynophagia. No history of smoking.\n\nPatient was in no acute distress. Neurologic examination revealed resting tremors in bilateral hands but was otherwise nonfocal. Abdominal examination was unremarkable.\n\n- Laboratory findings:  * Elevated serum creatinine: 3.7 mg/dL (327 μmol/L)  * Hemoglobin: 10.5 g/dL (105 g/L)  * α-fetoprotein and β-human chorionic gonadotropin levels: normal  - Imaging studies:  * CT head and C-spine: unremarkable  * CT chest: lobulated anterior mediastinal mass measuring 13.1 × 7.7 × 5.8 cm  * Contrast CT chest: showed lobar and segmental pulmonary emboli in right middle and lower lobes  * MRI brain with and without contrast: no intracranial mass lesion  - Biopsy results:  * Immunohistochemistry: Positive for P40 immunostain  * Negative for thyroid transcription factor-1 and napsin A immunostains  - Images:  * Figure 1 Title: Squamous cell carcinoma from a mediastinal mass  * Figure 2 Title: CT chest with IV contrast (axial view) showing anterior mediastinal mass and right-sided pulmonary embolism  * Figure 3 Title: CT chest with IV contrast (sagittal view) showing anterior mediastinal mass",
